**How to implement.** The library model in EF Core, holding slow data only (001 §C 'What it holds', 'What it never holds', 'Hybrid shape'; .spec-md §5 '`LibraryDbContext` + entities'). (1) New `OpticalConstructor.Database/LibraryEntities.cs`, namespace `OpticalConstructor.Database`: seven sealed entity classes with public get/set properties, each with a `<summary>` of one to three lines citing 001 §C and spec 0050. Their names carry `Library`, so no F# type name collides with them (ELEV014). (a) `LibraryEntryVersionRow`, one immutable version of a library entry: `Guid EntryId`, `int Version`, `int Kind` (the `LibraryEntryKind` tag), `string Name`, `int Lifecycle`, `int Protection`, `int PinState`, `int ProvenanceKind`, `Guid? CatalogueId` (the catalogue a built-in ships in, else null), `string PayloadText` (the entry file's canonical text, byte for byte) and `string ContentHash`. (b) `LibraryLatestVersionRow`, the local 'latest' of one entry: `Guid EntryId`, `int Version`. (c) `LibraryEntryFacetRow`: `Guid EntryId`, `int Version`, `string FacetKey`, `string FacetValue`. (d) `LibrarySampleMaterialRow`: `Guid SampleId`, `int SampleVersion`, `Guid MaterialId`, `int MaterialVersion`. (e) `LibraryCatalogueImportRow`, the catalogue-import log: `Guid CatalogueId`, `string Folder`, `string ManifestHash`, `DateTime ImportedAtUtc`, `int EntryCount`. (f) `LibraryPulledSourceRow`, a record pulled from an online source and the version it minted (001 §D): `Guid EntryId`, `int Version`, `string Source`, `string RecordPath`, `string SourceVersion` (a snapshot date or a commit), `string Licence`, `string Citation`. (g) `LibrarySchemaStampRow`: `int Id`, `int SchemaVersion`. Every string property is initialised, so nullable reference types raise no warning. (2) New `OpticalConstructor.Database/LibraryDbContext.cs`: `public class LibraryDbContext : DbContext` with the constructor `LibraryDbContext(DbContextOptions<LibraryDbContext> options)` and one `DbSet` per entity: `EntryVersions`, `LatestVersions`, `EntryFacets`, `SampleMaterials`, `CatalogueImports`, `PulledSources`, `SchemaStamps`. `OnModelCreating` pins with the fluent API: each table's name (`EntryVersion`, `LatestVersion`, `EntryFacet`, `SampleMaterial`, `CatalogueImport`, `PulledSource`, `SchemaStamp`); the keys, with every key property `ValueGeneratedNever()` so a key is always the caller's: `EntryVersion` (`EntryId`, `Version`), `LatestVersion` (`EntryId`), `EntryFacet` and `SampleMaterial` (all four columns), `CatalogueImport` (`CatalogueId`), `PulledSource` (`EntryId`, `Version`) and `SchemaStamp` (`Id`); every string column required; the foreign keys to `EntryVersion` (`EntryId`, `Version`): from `LatestVersion` with delete restricted, because a latest names a held version, and from `EntryFacet`, `PulledSource` and `SampleMaterial`'s (`SampleId`, `SampleVersion`) with delete cascading; and the indexes the queries read: `EntryVersion.Kind`, `EntryVersion.CatalogueId`, `EntryFacet` (`FacetKey`, `FacetValue`), `SampleMaterial` (`MaterialId`, `MaterialVersion`) and `PulledSource` (`Source`, `RecordPath`). A sample's material side is an indexed key, not a foreign key, so a sample version can be stored before the material versions it names. The model holds nothing a kit carries, no measured data and no preference (001 §C 'What it never holds'). The class doc says the database is created by `EnsureCreated` and stamped, never migrated (001 §C 'No migrations'). No migration, design-time factory, `HasData` seeding or second provider is added. (3) `OpticalConstructor.Storage.fsproj` gains `<ProjectReference Include="..\OpticalConstructor.Database\OpticalConstructor.Database.csproj" />` beside its Domain reference, with no comment. Storage owns the F# side of the database (.spec-md §5), and every Storage consumer reaches the model through it. No F# product code uses it in this step. Through Storage, Ui, the App, the TestWindows heads and both constructor test suites now carry EF Core, and each MUST build with zero warnings from our code, `MSB3277` included (AGENTS.md). (4) Tests: new `OpticalConstructor.Tests/LibraryDatabaseTests.fs` (`module LibraryDatabaseTests`), appended to the compile list with no comment. It reaches EF Core, `Microsoft.Data.Sqlite` and the model through Storage's reference, and its module doc is one to three lines citing spec 0050 S2.1. Fact `the library model creates exactly its seven tables and their columns`: over a `SqliteConnection` to `Data Source=:memory:` that the fact opens and disposes, `Database.EnsureCreated ()` of a `LibraryDbContext` built with `DbContextOptionsBuilder<LibraryDbContext>().UseSqlite(connection)` answers `true`. `sqlite_master` then lists exactly the tables `CatalogueImport`, `EntryFacet`, `EntryVersion`, `LatestVersion`, `PulledSource`, `SampleMaterial` and `SchemaStamp` (names starting `sqlite_` excluded), and each table's column names (`pragma_table_info`), compared as sets, are exactly those listed in (1). A second `EnsureCreated ()` answers `false`.

**Acceptance.** After the model lands, `OpticalConstructor.Database` MUST hold `LibraryDbContext` and its seven entities and no migration, and `OpticalConstructor.Storage.fsproj` MUST reference `OpticalConstructor.Database.csproj`; constructor-unit-tests MUST pass, including `the library model creates exactly its seven tables and their columns`; and build, elevation and the nine s08 invariants MUST pass.
